Cell["\<\
Use the cubic QED model for every diagram calculation and for four-point \
factorization. The model containing known four- and five-point amplitudes \
supplies five- and six-point factorization. \
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  4.003241769285503*^9},ExpressionUUID->"e40be2b8-a07c-4c47-93ff-\
40449897d0a0"],

Cell["\<\
diagramModel = ReadModel[FileNameJoin[{hepcatDirectory, \"models\"}], 2];
factorizationModel = ReadModel[FileNameJoin[{hepcatDirectory, \"models\"}], 3];
factorizationModelSpurious = ReadModel[FileNameJoin[{hepcatDirectory, \
\"models\"}], 4];
\
\>", "Input",
 CellChangeTimes->{{4.002815856960981*^9, 4.002815864610408*^9}},
 CellLabel->"In[4]:=",ExpressionUUID->"f61894d2-c74b-4610-a3de-e1941760c4ae"],

Cell["\<\
The independent benchmark uses the massive-QED Feynman model. Its own model \
file specifies the same numerical electron mass and charge as the \
constructive QED example. Each model retains its own parameters; no global \
parameter assignments or symbol substitutions are needed.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"5e949351-7167-4e81-83a5-e5d0eebf69c2"],

Cell["\<\
qedFeynmanModel = ReadModel[FileNameJoin[{hepcatDirectory, \
\"tests\", \"reference-models\", \"calchep\"}], 2];\
\>", "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[8]:=",ExpressionUUID->"39ecf8f2-3357-45c7-b69b-99fa03763f4d"],

Cell["\<\
The amplitudes retain symbolic Me and EE. Numerical checks and completion use \
the parameter values and derived functions from the supplied model. PASS \
means agreement at the tested samples, not a general analytic proof.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"43f1ccea-2be0-4dac-abdd-f6a191035779"],

Cell["\<\
The construction wrapper chooses its calculation settings automatically, \
reports progress and elapsed time, and returns the expression only when all \
required checks pass. The one option shown below supplies the independent \
Feynman benchmark model; it does not tune the calculation. Other options are \
needed only to override a default.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"ec7e2a65-5ad5-54a8-b037-0c605814a7c6"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["C++ Output", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"b4c6fb87-9ca2-562d-8135-105e4be086dd"],

Cell["\<\
The export cells at the end of each related amplitude family write one SPINAS \
process containing its requested spin and helicity choices. Evaluate the \
amplitude calculations first. Equivalent photon placements are supplied by \
relabeling identical particles. The generated amp selects one spin/helicity \
configuration; spin_sum sums its squared magnitude over all external states, \
and amp2 also includes the initial-state spin average and final-state \
identical-particle factor. Each export builds and runs three example \
phase-space points. Failed or unevaluated amplitudes stop the export before \
files are written. The process arrow separates incoming and outgoing \
particles. A generic A sums photon helicities; A.+ fixes a helicity on either \
side. Supply the original all-ingoing expressions: outgoing momenta and angle \
spinors are crossed automatically. Fixed incoming helicities are not \
averaged. The output folder is set below. Existing project files are \
protected; add \"Overwrite\" -> True only when you want to replace a previous \
export.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"434e8d10-f994-50ec-b513-28ad794a07e5"],

Cell["\<\
spinasOutputDirectory = FileNameJoin[{hepcatDirectory,\"tests\", \"results\", \
\"spinas-output\"}];\
\>", "Input",
 CellChangeTimes->{{4.0023639741745567`*^9, 4.002363979610005*^9}},
 CellLabel->"In[9]:=",ExpressionUUID->"bd44a057-8980-5237-a7d8-0b841566c656"],

Cell["\<\
Choose the independent reference programs and models here. These are explicit \
example locations; change them for your installation. The selected MadGraph \
SM model is restricted to electrons, positrons and photons by the visible PDG \
list. CalcHEP uses the separate massive-QED Feynman model. The checks compare \
squared amplitudes, so overall phases are allowed. Each individual \
calculation has a MadGraph check at its specified photon helicities, with \
massive spins summed. Each grouped C++ export also runs the full CalcHEP \
spin/helicity-summed comparison and the MadGraph helicity checks. All use \
at least 100,000 random points per numerical comparison and print the seed for replay. Failed or \
unfinished constructive calculations are never counted as passing.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"26c8f871-c0fb-5a55-a4c8-8bc257b7184b"],

Cell["\<\
madGraphDirectory = FileNameJoin[{ParentDirectory[hepcatDirectory], \
\"MadGraph\"}];
madGraphModel = \"sm-full\";
calcHEPDirectory = FileNameJoin[{ParentDirectory[hepcatDirectory], \
\"CalcHEP\", \"calchep_3.9.2\"}];
calcHEPModel = {FileNameJoin[{hepcatDirectory, \"tests\", \"reference-models\", \"calchep\"}], 2};
referenceParticlePDGs = {11, 22};
validationPoints = 100000;
madGraphValidationOptions = {
  \"MadGraphDirectory\" -> madGraphDirectory,
  \"MadGraphModel\" -> madGraphModel,
  \"MadGraphAllowedParticles\" -> referenceParticlePDGs,
  \"MadGraphPoints\" -> validationPoints
};\
\>", "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[10]:=",ExpressionUUID->"c44af2bb-0ad6-562d-8a65-8ae930fd2c08"],

Cell["The 7 catalog processes each use 100,000 physical momenta. All 1,400,000 complete spin and helicity sums agree with CalcHEP at relative tolerance 1e-7. At fixed photon helicity, 50 of 7,200,000 double-precision comparisons exceed that tolerance against MadGraph. All come from the four-photon spurious-pole amplitude with one opposite helicity. The four-photon precision study evaluates all 16 helicity assignments again at all 100,000 points.", "Text", CellTags->"QEDCurrentCatalogSummary"],

Cell["The tables below load the current results and sample sizes from the accompanying benchmark reports. Earlier output cells elsewhere in this notebook retain the counts from the runs that produced them.", "Text"],

Cell["With[{file = FileNameJoin[{hepcatDirectory,\"tests\",\"benchmarks\",\"reproduction\",\"100k\",\"summary.json\"}]},\n  Dataset[Import[file,\"RawJSON\"][\"studies\"]]\n]", "Input", CellTags->"QEDPublicationValidationSummary"],

Cell["General-formula checks use fixed photon helicities and sum the massive spins. The table gives the native double-precision comparisons with MadGraph for one negative photon and for two fermion pairs with positive photons. The last column counts distinct points above tolerance in any main check, including reference, permutation and Ward tests. The separately reported individual-spin Ward diagnostic is excluded from that column.", "Text"],

Cell["Module[{directory, rows},\n  directory = FileNameJoin[{hepcatDirectory, \"tests\", \"benchmarks\", \"reproduction\", \"100k\", \"general\", \"results\"}];\n  rows[label_, prefix_, multiplicities_] := Table[\n    With[{r = Import[FileNameJoin[{directory, prefix <> \"-N\" <> ToString[n] <> \".json\"}], \"RawJSON\"]},\n      <|\"Family and sample\" -> label, \"Photons\" -> n, \"Points\" -> r[\"Points\"],\n        \"Seed\" -> r[\"Seed\"], \"Relative tolerance\" -> r[\"RelativeTolerance\"],\n        \"Maximum vs MadGraph\" -> r[\"Checks\"][\"MadGraph\"][\"MaximumRelativeError\"],\n        \"Above tolerance vs MadGraph\" -> r[\"Checks\"][\"MadGraph\"][\"Failures\"],\n        \"Above tolerance in any main check\" -> r[\"FailedPoints\"]|>\n    ], {n, multiplicities}];\n  Dataset[Join[\n    rows[\"One negative, first sample\", \"minus\", Range[2, 8]],\n    rows[\"One negative, second sample\", \"minus-secondary\", Range[2, 8]],\n    rows[\"Two pairs, all positive\", \"two\", Range[0, 5]]\n  ]]\n]", "Input", CellTags->"QEDGeneralFamilyMadGraphResults"],

Cell["Finite one-pair formulas for three and four photons with one opposite helicity. The checks and exports in the corresponding sections use this function.", "Text"],

Cell["(* Literal mixed-helicity formulas in Sections 3.2 and 3.5.\n   Canonical photon labels are mapped to the requested legs before evaluating each chain. *)\nqedFiniteMixedPhotonAmplitude[ends_List, like_List, opposite_, mass_, charge_, poleForm_, parity_:False] :=\n Module[{labels = Join[ends,like,{opposite}], leg, chirality, spin, ag, sq, x, ac, rt, den,\n   t, s345, ww, zz, fphys, fspur, cycles, common, spinTerm, physicalTerms, firstNumerator,\n   secondNumerator, numerator, sphys, js, sspur},\n leg[k_] := labels[[k]];\n chirality[t_] := If[TrueQ[parity], If[t===\"Angle\",\"Square\",\"Angle\"], t];\n(* Spinor products, ordered chains and physical fermion denominators appearing in the paper. *)\nspin[t_,k_] := Spinor[If[k<=2,\"Spin\",\"Helicity\"], chirality[t], leg[k]];\nag[i_,j_]:=SpinorChain[spin[\"Angle\",i],spin[\"Angle\",j]];\nsq[i_,j_]:=SpinorChain[spin[\"Square\",i],spin[\"Square\",j]];\nx[i_,p_,j_]:=SpinorChain[spin[\"Angle\",i],Mom[leg[p]],spin[\"Square\",j]];\nac[h_]:=SpinorChain[spin[\"Angle\",h],Mom[leg[1]],Mom[leg[2]],spin[\"Angle\",h]];\nrt[k_]:=SpinorChain[spin[\"Square\",k],Mom[leg[1]],Mom[leg[2]],spin[\"Square\",k]];\nden[i_,j_]:=Total[SpinorChain[spin[\"Square\",i],Mom[leg[1]],Mom[leg[#]],spin[\"Square\",j]]& /@ {i,5}];\nt[p_,a__]:=PropDen[Mom[Multiparticle@@Sort[leg /@ {p,a}]],mass];\ns345 := PropDen[Mom[Multiparticle @@ Sort[leg /@ {3,4,5}]],0]; ww[h_]:=ag[1,h]ag[2,h]; zz := ag[1,2];\n\n(* Three photons. *)\nfphys := 2 Sqrt[2]charge^3/(mass t[1,3]t[1,4]t[1,5])(\n  mass^2 sq[3,4]^2/t[2,5]((t[1,5]+t[2,5])ww[5]+zz ac[5])\n  +x[5,1,4]rt[3]/t[2,3](zz x[5,1,4]-sq[4,5]ww[5])\n  +x[5,1,3]rt[4]/t[2,4](zz x[5,1,3]-sq[3,5]ww[5]));\nfspur := 2 Sqrt[2]charge^3/mass Total[Function[ij,With[{i=First[ij],j=Last[ij]},(1+t[1,i]/t[1,5]+t[2,j]/t[2,5])(\n x[5,1,i]x[5,2,j]/(t[1,i]t[2,j]ag[5,3]ag[5,4]) (ww[5]-zz x[5,1,i]x[5,2,j]/den[i,j])-\n mass^2 zz sq[i,j]^4/(s345 sq[i,5]sq[5,j]den[i,j]))]]/@{{3,4},{4,3}}];\n(* Four photons. The three cyclic choices of j,b,c give six\n   physical-pole terms and the two terms in the common numerator. *)\ncycles = {{3,4,5},{4,5,3},{5,3,4}};\ncommon := t[1,3]t[1,4]t[1,5]t[1,6];\nspinTerm[j_] := sq[j,6]ww[6] - zz x[6,1,j];\nphysicalTerms[j_,b_,c_] := (\n mass sq[b,c]^2 x[6,1,j]spinTerm[j]/(t[1,j]t[1,6]t[2,b]t[2,c]t[1,j,6])\n - mass sq[b,c]^2 x[6,2,j](sq[j,6]ww[6]+zz x[6,2,j])/(t[1,b]t[1,c]t[2,6]t[2,j]t[1,b,c]));\nfirstNumerator[j_,b_,c_] := sq[b,c]^2 rt[j] t[2,b]t[2,c];\nsecondNumerator[j_,b_,c_] := t[2,j] x[6,1,j]spinTerm[j] rt[b]rt[c];\nnumerator := (mass^2((t[1,6]+t[2,6])ww[6]+zz ac[6]) Total[firstNumerator @@@ cycles]\n - t[2,6] Total[secondNumerator @@@ cycles]);\nsphys := 4charge^4 (Total[physicalTerms @@@ cycles]\n + numerator/(mass common t[2,6]t[2,3]t[2,4]t[2,5]));\n\n(* Three positive photons are partitioned among the two line endpoints. *)\njs[a_,{}]=1;\njs[a_,{i_}]:=x[6,a,i]/(ag[6,i]t[a,i]);\njs[a_,{i_,j_}]:=(x[6,a,i]x[6,a,j]/(ag[6,i]ag[6,j])-mass^2 sq[i,j]^2/t[a,i,j])/(t[a,i]t[a,j]);\njs[a_,{3,4,5}]:=Total[Function[ijk,With[{i=ijk[[1]],j=ijk[[2]],k=ijk[[3]]},js[a,{i,j}] (x[6,a,k]+ag[6,i]sq[i,k]+ag[6,j]sq[j,k])/ag[6,k]]]/@{{3,4,5},{4,5,3},{5,3,4}}]/t[a,3,4,5];\nsspur := 4charge^4/mass Total[Function[l,With[{r=Complement[{3,4,5},l]},(-1)^Length[r]js[1,l]js[2,r](zz (x[6,1,3]+Total[(ag[6,#]sq[#,3])&/@l])/sq[6,3]+ww[6])]]/@Subsets[{3,4,5}]];\n Switch[{Length[like],poleForm},\n   {2,\"AllPhysical\"},fphys, {2,\"AllSpurious\"},fspur,\n   {3,\"AllPhysical\"},sphys, {3,\"AllSpurious\"},sspur, _,$Failed]\n]", "Input", CellTags->"QEDFiniteMixedDefinition"],

Cell["The balanced four-photon amplitude uses the same pair-emission currents in its physical and single-bracket gauges.", "Text"],

Cell["(* Combine the two emission orders at each end of a massive fermion line.\n   positive and negative contain the two external labels of each helicity.\n   couplingProduct is the product of the four stripped cubic vertex factors. *)\nqedBalancedPhotonCurrent[endpoints_List, positive_List, negative_List, mass_, couplingProduct_, gauge_:\"Physical\"] := Module[\n  {left, right, photons, allLegs, spin, bracket, angle, square, sandwich, pole, emission,\n   pairContact, determinant, oneCurrent, twoCurrent, contract, bridge, contact, pairs},\n  {left, right} = endpoints;\n  photons = Join[positive, negative];\n  allLegs = Sort[Join[endpoints, photons]];\n  spin[type_, leg_] := Spinor[If[MemberQ[endpoints, leg], \"Spin\", \"Helicity\"], type, leg];\n  bracket[type_, i_, j_] := If[i === j, 0, SpinorChain[spin[type, i], spin[type, j]]];\n  angle[i_, j_] := bracket[\"Angle\", i, j];\n  square[i_, j_] := bracket[\"Square\", i, j];\n  sandwich[i_, p_, j_] := SpinorChain[spin[\"Square\", i], Mom[p], spin[\"Angle\", j]];\n  pole[legs__] := With[{cut = Sort[{legs}]},\n    PropDen[Mom[Multiparticle @@ First[SortBy[{cut, Complement[allLegs, cut]}, {Length[#], #} &]]], mass]];\n\n  (* In the single-bracket gauge the positive photons use the left endpoint,\n     the first negative photon the right endpoint, and the last the first positive photon. *)\n  emission[i_, p_] := Which[\n    gauge === \"SingleBracket\" && i === Last[negative],\n      sandwich[First[positive], p, i]/square[First[positive], i],\n    gauge === \"SingleBracket\" && i === First[negative],\n      If[p === right, 0, SpinorChain[spin[\"Angle\", i], Mom[right], Mom[p], spin[\"Angle\", i]]/pole[right, i]],\n    p === left, 0,\n    MemberQ[positive, i], SpinorChain[spin[\"Square\", i], Mom[p], Mom[left], spin[\"Square\", i]]/pole[left, i],\n    True, SpinorChain[spin[\"Angle\", i], Mom[left], Mom[p], spin[\"Angle\", i]]/pole[left, i]];\n  determinant[p_, i_, j_] := Which[MemberQ[positive, j], -mass^2 square[i, j]^2,\n    MemberQ[positive, i], sandwich[i, p, j]^2, True, -mass^2 angle[i, j]^2];\n  oneCurrent[p_, i_] := Prepend[(-Boole[i === #] angle[#, p] &) /@ negative, emission[i, p]];\n  twoCurrent[p_, {i_, j_}] := Module[{central = pole[p, i, j], spinTerms},\n    spinTerms = Which[MemberQ[positive, j], {0, 0}, MemberQ[positive, i],\n      (-Boole[# === j] angle[j, p] (emission[i, p] central + square[i, j] sandwich[i, p, j]) &) /@ negative,\n      True, Function[k, -angle[k, p] emission[If[k === i, j, i], p] central\n        + mass angle[i, j]^2 square[k, p]] /@ negative];\n    Prepend[spinTerms, emission[i, p] emission[j, p] central + determinant[p, i, j]]/\n      (pole[p, i] pole[p, j])];\n  contract[l_, r_] := l . Outer[angle, Prepend[negative, left], Prepend[negative, right]] . r;\n  pairs = Subsets[photons, {2}];\n  bridge[ij_] := With[{kl = Select[photons, !MemberQ[ij, #] &]},\n    contract[twoCurrent[left, ij], twoCurrent[right, kl]]/pole[left, Sequence @@ ij]];\n  pairContact[i_, j_] := Which[\n    gauge === \"Physical\" || MemberQ[positive, j], -determinant[left, i, j]/(pole[left, i] pole[left, j]),\n    MemberQ[positive, i] && j === First[negative],\n      -sandwich[i, left, j] sandwich[i, right, j]/(pole[left, i] pole[right, j]),\n    MemberQ[positive, i],\n      sandwich[i, left, j] square[i, First[positive]]/(pole[left, i] square[First[positive], j]),\n    True, -angle[i, j] SpinorChain[spin[\"Angle\", i], Mom[right], spin[\"Square\", First[positive]]]/\n      (pole[right, i] square[First[positive], j])];\n  contact[ij_] := With[{kl = Select[photons, !MemberQ[ij, #] &]},\n    (pairContact @@ ij) *\n      Total[Function[order, With[{k = First[order], l = Last[order]},\n        contract[oneCurrent[left, k], oneCurrent[right, l]]/(pole[left, k] pole[right, l])]] /@ Permutations[kl]]];\n  -couplingProduct Total[(bridge[#] + contact[#]) & /@ pairs]/mass\n];", "Input", CellTags->"QEDBalancedGaugeDefinition"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],

